WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:05.960
Let's start with a question: could the war
in Ukraine cause a famine in Africa?

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:12.570
I know what many of you are thinking: Russia
and Ukraine are over here on the eastern edge

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:16.380
of Europe while the countries of Africa are
way down here on this large land mass.

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:20.320
But, the reality, VisualPolitik viewers, is
that the war has turned the world upside down.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:24.010
We have already told you in a lot of videos
how it has caused chaos in the energy markets.

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:25.650
However, that is not the only consequence.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:30.470
The war in Ukraine is also putting another
very important market, the agricultural market,

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:31.470
on the ropes.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:34.309
Think about it, it is very difficult to live
without oil, without gas, or with the prices

00:00:34.309 --> 00:00:38.620
of these hydrocarbons skyrocketing, but living
without wheat or corn is not easier either.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:41.910
In fact, for millions and millions of people,
the lack of these foods could mean the difference

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:43.690
between eating or going hungry.

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:46.740
And you will probably say: OK, but... What
do Russia and Ukraine have to do with all

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:50.480
this? Well, more than you might think. Basically,
because we are talking about two of the largest

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.309
agricultural producers in the world.

00:00:52.309 --> 00:00:55.129
I’m sure that many of you who are watching
us from Europe, from the United States or

00:00:55.129 --> 00:00:59.109
even from Latin America have noticed that
food prices have gone up a lot during the

00:00:59.109 --> 00:01:02.540
last year. Well, you already know what one
of the main reasons is.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:06.439
It's certainly been tough, but fortunately,
in general, in most parts of the world we

00:01:06.439 --> 00:01:11.320
are not starving. However, there is one part
of the world where the same cannot be said:

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:15.230
sub-Saharan Africa.

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:19.009
We have told you many times here on VisualPolitik
how in the world, and particularly in sub-Saharan

00:01:19.009 --> 00:01:22.900
Africa, so much progress has been made in
recent years that, for many people, famines

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:27.020
were doomed to be nothing more than bad chapters
in the history books. And indeed, famine did

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:28.970
seem to be on the road to extinction.

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:32.899
The Russian and Ukrainian agricultural sectors
have been key players in this. To give you

00:01:32.899 --> 00:01:37.220
an idea, Ukraine produces enough food for
400 million people every year, many of them

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:38.400
in Sub-Saharan Africa.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:42.590
But now the war has complicated everything.
Grain exports from Ukraine and Russia are

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:46.590
no longer as large and frequent as they were
before Putin launched his troops on his neighboring

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:50.800
country. And naturally, this has caused tension
in agricultural markets to skyrocket.

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:54.580
And not just because Ukrainian Black Sea ports
are constantly targeted by Russia or because

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:58.610
fighting has limited Ukraine's production,
but because even sanctions on Russia now make

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:01.009
its exports much more difficult and more expensive.

00:02:01.009 --> 00:02:05.299
The result? The likelihood of an uncontrolled
food crisis has not been this high since the

00:02:05.299 --> 00:02:06.299
1980s.

00:02:06.299 --> 00:02:10.640
So, the question is: what initiatives are
being promoted to avoid a catastrophic famine?

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:13.740
Why were Russia and Ukraine selling so much
wheat to Africa?

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:17.660
And two other questions that may surprise
you even more: What is Turkey's role in all

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:21.519
this story? Could Turkey succeed in preventing
a worldwide famine? Today we are going to

00:02:21.519 --> 00:02:31.140
answer these questions, but first let's look
at a bit of history.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:35.000
( TECHNOLOGY, PRODUCTION AND HARD WORK)

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:39.830
If you are faithful followers of VisualPolitik,
you will remember that one of the things we

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:43.420
have repeated ad nauseam on this channel is
that the number of people suffering from poverty

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:47.910
and going hungry has been plummeting around
the world over the last few decades.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:51.830
So far this century, extreme hunger has ceased
to be commonplace. And that is mainly due

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:54.379
to one reason: technological advances.

00:02:54.379 --> 00:02:57.940
And, remember that if I were to ask you which
economic sector has developed the most in

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:01.409
recent decades, I’m pretty sure most of
you would automatically think of industry,

00:03:01.409 --> 00:03:05.580
IT or something like that. Am I wrong? I'm
sure I'm not.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:10.060
And in a way you are right... But do you know
what? The evolution of the agricultural sector

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:14.129
is no less impressive. To give you an idea,
at the beginning of the 20th century, in Europe

00:03:14.129 --> 00:03:17.780
or in the United States, we could still find
images like this one:

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:22.909
However, today the fields of the most developed
countries increasingly resemble this:Exactly,

00:03:22.909 --> 00:03:27.360
it is increasingly common to see how driverless
tractors, irrigation robots, drones and many

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:31.790
other technological advances allow us to produce
much more food every year. Not to mention

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:35.959
genetically modified foods. The truth is that,
in the coming decades, we may see an enormous

00:03:35.959 --> 00:03:37.280
growth in food production.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.691
A leap that we already experienced a few decades
ago. Between the 1960s and the 1980s, there

00:03:41.691 --> 00:03:46.799
was a 180º turnaround in agricultural markets.
Of course, I am talking about the so-called

00:03:46.799 --> 00:03:51.720
Green Revolution.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:55.610
The Green Revolution was nothing more than
a gigantic increase in agricultural productivity

00:03:55.610 --> 00:04:00.220
on a global scale. It began in Mexico and
the United States at the end of the 1950s

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:03.940
with the wheat revolution, and gradually spread
throughout the world. The increase in productivity

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:07.519
came about thanks to the use of new technologies,
the planting of cereals resistant to the most

00:04:07.519 --> 00:04:11.970
extreme climates and pests, and the use of
synthetic fertilizers and pesticides. As a

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:16.160
result, countries in Latin America and throughout
Asia, such as Mexico, Pakistan and India,

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:22.759
were able to multiply their production.
Another well-known case was that of China,

00:04:22.759 --> 00:04:26.320
a country where the green revolution, along
with the pro-market reforms implemented by

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:30.330
Deng Xiaoping, succeeded in reducing famine
levels to historic lows.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:34.800
We could say that the Green Revolution left
even the most faithful Malthusian trembling,

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:38.430
since it clearly proved that the increase
in production is fully compatible with the

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:42.639
increase in population and that world hunger
could be eradicated.

00:04:42.639 --> 00:04:46.419
And not only that, it also confirmed another
trend that is now more than evident: that

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:50.400
higher production does not mean more people
working in agriculture.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.530
In fact, as you can see, fewer and fewer people
are working in the sector, and at the same

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:56.320
time, production is increasing.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:01.810
However, wait a moment, because not all of
the process was so successful.

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:04.860
Sub-Saharan Africa was the only region of
the world that could not successfully join

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:12.180
the Green Revolution.
Why? Well for several reasons.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:15.510
The first and perhaps most important is that,
while the rest of the countries were already

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:19.180
independent during the Green Revolution, many
of the sub-Saharan African countries were

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:21.300
still struggling to free themselves.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:24.940
And, when they did succeed, internal power
struggles and corrupt governments began to

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:28.090
flourish, making it virtually impossible to
develop infrastructure projects that would

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:32.040
allow production to be increased and then
transported from one place to another. Of

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:35.290
course, investment and agricultural development
need sufficient infrastructure to be able

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:36.870
to quickly export their production.

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:40.530
That’s not all though, for example, this
same lack of infrastructure is also one of

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:44.940
the reasons why fertilizer use is so low in
the region, because transportation costs are

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:45.990
much higher.

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:50.320
And if we add to all this the extreme climatic
conditions in many areas of sub-Saharan Africa,

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:53.840
then we have the perfect cocktail to explain
the underdevelopment of this sector in the

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:54.840
region.

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:58.340
This is precisely what explains, to a large
extent, why subsistence agriculture is still

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:01.930
the order of the day here and why many of
the countries in the region have to import

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:03.069
so much food.

00:06:03.069 --> 00:06:06.880
And as the population keeps growing... Then
the problem is clear. The demand for food

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:08.380
imports is increasing.

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:13.190
And of course, faced with such a scenario,
the Ukrainian war has set that ticking time

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:14.460
bomb off.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:18.300
VisualPolitik viewers, this is precisely the
number one reason why the Ukrainian war has

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:22.630
become such a huge nightmare for the forgotten
continent.

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:33.130
Listen up.
(FINANCIAL INNOVATION AGAINST FAMINE)

00:06:33.130 --> 00:06:36.580
Remember the anti-globalization activists?
At the beginning of the 21st century we had

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:40.370
many people saying things like that financial
globalization was going to completely destroy

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:44.650
the Third World and that the differences between
rich and poor countries would increase catastrophically.

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:47.660
Well, nothing could be further from the truth.

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:52.120
In this video we have already told you how
technological evolution has multiplied agricultural

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:55.840
production to the point of drastically reducing
famines around the world.

00:06:55.840 --> 00:07:00.120
But what we have not yet told you is how financial
innovations have also helped to end hunger

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:03.720
in the world. We are talking specifically
about the so-called "agricultural futures

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:04.720
market".

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:08.970
And you may be wondering, what are these markets?
Well, basically, they are markets where you

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:13.120
can buy and sell, very easily, agricultural
products, such as wheat or corn, without having

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:16.500
to move them all the time. They are instruments
that allow you to guarantee your food supply

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:19.590
in the future. That is why they are called
futures.

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:23.440
Of course, these markets are not strictly
new, far from it. They have been with us much

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:28.380
longer than you might imagine.

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:31.810
To give you an idea, the first agricultural
futures market was registered in the United

00:07:31.810 --> 00:07:37.440
States, in 1848, in the Chicago Board of Trade.
But it was not until the 1960s and 1970s that

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:42.620
we could speak of a truly global and totally
open market in which to buy and sell agricultural

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:43.620
products.

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:47.710
As production could be sold in advance, investment
in the sector increased and agricultural exports

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:57.651
soared.
And so a new global market was born. Thanks

00:07:57.651 --> 00:08:01.130
to it, developing countries that find it more
difficult to produce their own food were able

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:05.000
to access a lot of new, much cheaper food.
In other words, the countries of sub-Saharan

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:08.470
Africa that could not get on board with the
Green Revolution were able to make up for

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:12.210
their production shortages by buying on the
international market. And that contributed

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:14.520
decisively to reducing famines.

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:18.500
This does not mean that they have disappeared,
for example, right now there is the dramatic

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:22.160
famine that Somalia is going through as a
result of a severe ongoing drought, which

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:25.190
has already claimed more than 43,000 lives.

00:08:25.190 --> 00:08:28.410
On the other hand, the dependence of these
countries on wheat imports also makes them

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:33.019
more vulnerable to international market fluctuations.
We saw this in the 2008 financial crisis,

00:08:33.019 --> 00:08:37.159
which led to a food crisis, and we saw it
again in 2020 with the Covid-19 crisis and

00:08:37.159 --> 00:08:38.510
the collapse of supply chains.

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:41.950
But now the situation is even more dramatic.
Take a look at the countries which are the

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:47.910
world's leading grain exporters:
Exactly, VisualPolitik viewers, Russia is

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:51.840
the world's largest grain exporter and Ukraine
the fifth largest. And we are not talking

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:56.050
only about grains, they are also huge exporters
of many other products. Take the case of sunflower

00:08:56.050 --> 00:08:59.990
oil, Ukraine had a 46% world market share
of this staple before the war.

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:03.700
Their black soils, very rich in nutrients,
along with reforms since the fall of the Soviet

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:07.050
Union, have led these two countries to increase
their agricultural production to the point

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:10.910
of becoming the granaries of a large part
of Europe and the world.

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:14.720
Without going any further, countries such
as Benin, Tanzania and Congo import more than

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:17.680
70% of their grain from Russia.

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:21.089
Precisely for this reason, the all-out war
that is now pitting Russia against Ukraine

00:09:21.089 --> 00:09:25.560
has set off all the alarm bells. Prices have
skyrocketed and food insecurity has once again

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:26.970
become a hot topic.

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:30.550
The question, then, is what is being done
to avoid another humanitarian catastrophe

00:09:30.550 --> 00:09:37.700
in sub-Saharan Africa? How is an attempt being
made to tame prices? Well, let's take a look.

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:46.370
(AND TURKEY... WHAT'S IT DOING HERE?)

00:09:46.370 --> 00:09:49.940
We have already told you that Ukraine was,
and still is, one of the largest grain producers

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:55.329
in the world. Indeed, 72% of its entire rural
population works in agriculture. But the war

00:09:55.329 --> 00:09:57.399
has been devastating for this sector:

00:09:57.399 --> 00:10:01.089
Between shelling, forced conscription and
Russia's naval dominance in the Black Sea,

00:10:01.089 --> 00:10:04.961
production costs have gone through the roof.
In addition, it is estimated that 25% of all

00:10:04.961 --> 00:10:08.140
Ukrainian households living on agriculture
have suspended their activities.

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:12.930
However, it is not only Ukraine that has problems.
Russia is not spared either. True, Western

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:16.649
countries did not impose sanctions against
its agricultural sector, and in principle

00:10:16.649 --> 00:10:20.340
they should have a much easier time exporting
to the rest of the world, since they dominate

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:21.340
the Black Sea.

00:10:21.340 --> 00:10:25.120
However, the sanctions are affecting them
indirectly. Being excluded from international

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:28.930
payment systems, having their logistics sanctioned
and insurers refusing to work with Russian

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:32.250
cargoes, their agricultural sector is suffering
like the Ukrainian one.

00:10:32.250 --> 00:10:35.270
Surely now you can better understand a graph
like this one.

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:39.029
So in case you’re wondering, that’s why
bread and baked goods were suddenly more expensive

00:10:39.029 --> 00:10:40.970
when you went to the bakery…

00:10:40.970 --> 00:10:45.209
Obviously, it's not just about bread... It's
also about the bulk of the food basket. And,

00:10:45.209 --> 00:10:48.620
of course, what may be an uncomfortable price
increase in Europe or the United States, can

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:52.899
mean the difference between eating or starving
in regions like sub-Saharan Africa.

00:10:52.899 --> 00:10:57.260
And so, the international community began
to call for changes to avoid a food catastrophe.

00:10:57.260 --> 00:11:04.950
And do you know what? This is when our old
friend, Turkey, enters the picture.

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:09.279
Turkey's role in this war between Ukraine
and Russia has been ambivalent to say the

00:11:09.279 --> 00:11:17.630
least: it has not sanctioned Russia, but neither
has it supported it, as it is a member of

00:11:17.630 --> 00:11:20.480
NATO. And the country has even supplied weapons
to the Ukrainian army.

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:24.160
But beyond that, what is certain is that in
general terms it has sought to play a certain

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:27.839
neutral role. Turkey has taken advantage of
this conflict to make a move on the international

00:11:27.839 --> 00:11:32.200
chessboard and try to win greater international
sympathy by presenting itself as the mediator

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:34.870
between the two countries.
And of course, being on the Black Sea and

00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:38.550
in control of the Bosphorus Strait, Turkey
has played a key role in trying to restore

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:42.170
grain exports from the Black Sea. In fact,
together with the United Nations, it has been

00:11:42.170 --> 00:11:46.700
the forerunner of a crucial initiative to
reduce world grain prices: the Black Sea Grain

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:49.590
Initiative.

00:11:49.590 --> 00:11:53.510
And what does this initiative consist of?
Well, very simple: Russia does not bomb Ukrainian

00:11:53.510 --> 00:11:57.620
ships carrying grain from the Black Sea; Ukraine
enables three ports to carry out its exports,

00:11:57.620 --> 00:12:01.950
Odessa, Chornomorsk and Yuzhne; and Turkey
checks all vessels and cargoes in Istanbul

00:12:01.950 --> 00:12:06.100
when Ukrainian ships enter and leave to prevent
them, for example, from carrying weapons.

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:10.540
Sounds good, doesn't it? Well, you have to
admit that the Initiative has been quite successful.

00:12:10.540 --> 00:12:16.399
As you can see, both wheat and corn prices
have been falling since the initiative was

00:12:16.399 --> 00:12:19.860
signed. However, there have been moments of
great uncertainty because at times Russia

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:23.140
has given the impression that it wants to
break this agreement... This has caused prices

00:12:23.140 --> 00:12:24.910
to rebound every now and then.

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:28.750
And the fact is that Moscow is playing with
this initiative. They are trying to use it

00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:30.370
to get some sanctions eased.

00:12:30.370 --> 00:12:35.220
For example, when Putin signed his participation
in July 2022, he did so for four months, and

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:39.250
in November, when he renewed it, he did the
same, but just recently on 18 March 2023,

00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:43.160
when he had to renew it, he did so for only
60 days. Why? Because he wants to tighten

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:44.880
the screws on Western countries.

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:49.070
And not only that. Russia wants to exploit
this position intensively. Just look.

00:12:49.070 --> 00:12:54.100
("If we finally decide not to extend the agreement
within sixty days, we are ready to supply

00:12:54.100 --> 00:12:58.709
from Russia free of charge to these countries
all the volume sent during the last time to

00:12:58.709 --> 00:13:03.279
the African countries in special need" – V.
Putin (March 2023)

00:13:03.279 --> 00:13:06.529
We can't be sure that this is going to be
the case until Russia decides to withdraw

00:13:06.529 --> 00:13:10.440
from the initiative, if it does pull out.
But giving something away for free... I don't

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:17.630
know, it doesn't seem very Russian. They always
want something in return.

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:21.529
We have already told you about Russia's strategy
in Africa, and how the Russian mercenary group

00:13:21.529 --> 00:13:26.260
Wagner, closely linked to the Kremlin, does
not provide its services altruistically: gold

00:13:26.260 --> 00:13:27.260
always comes first.

00:13:27.260 --> 00:13:31.430
But even if it were willing to do so, it would
be very difficult, if not impossible, to replace

00:13:31.430 --> 00:13:35.610
Ukrainian production. This is why this Initiative
is crucial for all parties: not only to lower

00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:39.240
prices on international markets, but also
to avoid a new food catastrophe in the most

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:42.660
vulnerable regions, as is precisely the case
in sub-Saharan Africa.

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:46.209
The problem is that having Russia playing
with the deadlines for extending its participation

00:13:46.209 --> 00:13:50.620
not only generates great uncertainty in the
markets, but also in the daily lives of hundreds

00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:54.990
of millions of people. At the moment these
are the expectations for 2023 in a market

00:13:54.990 --> 00:13:57.180
that is already very pressured:

00:13:57.180 --> 00:14:03.510
(The world cereal harvest will fall by 2.2
% in the 2022-2023 campaign, according to

00:14:03.510 --> 00:14:04.510
the US. EFEAGRO)

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:08.079
So hold on, it’s going to be a bumpy road
ahead. But at this point, the questions are

00:14:08.079 --> 00:14:09.410
for you:

00:14:09.410 --> 00:14:13.470
Do you think Russia will break its participation
in the Grain Initiative? Will we see another

00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:17.459
major food crisis again? What solutions do
you think could be viable for alleviating

00:14:17.459 --> 00:14:19.720
the situation? You can leave me your answer
in the comments.

